      "math_academy_id": "q-184078",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Suppose $n=20{m}^{5}+16m-5,$ where $m\\in Z.$ Complete the following argument.\n\nThe number $n$ is {{field-1}} since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =20{m}^{5}+16m-5 \\\\  & =2(10{m}^{5}+{{field-2}})+1 \\\\  & ={{field-3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=$ {{field-4}} is an integer.",
      "worked_solution": "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a+1,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nNow, note the following regarding the expression $20{m}^{5}+16m-5:$\n\n- All terms, apart from the constant term, have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- The constant term can be written as $-5=-6+1.$\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is $\\text{odd}$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =20{m}^{5}+16m-5 \\\\  & =(20{m}^{5}+16m-6)+1 \\\\  & =2({{10{m}^{5}+8m-3}_{⏟}}_{a})+1 \\\\  & =2a+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=10{m}^{5}+8m-3$ is an integer.",
